1. DEFINITIONS
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------

1.1 "Licensor" means the copyright owner(s) of Morbi Engine who distribute the
    Engine under this License.

1.2 "You" (or "Your") means the individual or legal entity exercising
    permissions granted by this License.

1.3 "Engine" means the original Morbi Engine software, including its C++ source
    code, headers, shaders, C# managed bridge source code, editor source code,
    build scripts, project templates, documentation, and original assets made
    available by Licensor under this License, expressly excluding Third-Party
    Components.

1.4 "Modifications" means any additions, deletions, patches, forks, or
    derivative works You make to the Engine.

1.5 "Engine Development Tools" means MorbiEditor, the Editor Frontend, CMake
    build scripts, SDK headers, static import libraries used for engine
    development, LauncherTemplate source, ProjectBuilder, shader compilation
    toolchains, and any development-only dependencies or utilities packaged
    with or used to build the Engine.

1.6 "Engine Runtime Components" means solely the compiled binary files of the
    Engine (such as compiled dynamic/shared libraries, static libraries linked
    into a game executable, compiled managed runtime assemblies, dedicated
    server binaries, and the compiled game launcher executable) that are
    strictly necessary at runtime to execute a Game Build.

1.7 "Game" means an interactive video game, simulation, or 3D end-user
    application developed by You using the Engine, whose primary purpose is
    end-user entertainment, visualization, or simulation, and NOT to function as
    a game engine, rendering engine, authoring tool, editor, SDK, middleware,
    or software development framework.

1.8 "Game Build" means a compiled, packaged end-user distribution of Your Game
    (including an associated dedicated game server build for Your Game)
    intended solely for end users to play or run Your Game.

1.9 "Third-Party Components" means any third-party libraries, runtimes,
    toolchains, packages, or assets referenced, vendored, fetched, or linked by
    the Engine, as documented in docs/THIRD_PARTY_NOTICES.md.

